<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zlín</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zlín is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. From the 1890s it grew from a small town into an industrial centre as the home of the Bata shoe company, which rebuilt it in a modernist style with brick factories and workers' housing. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> it was named Gottwaldov from 1949 to 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zlin.jpg" alt="Zlín" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velecky · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscraper_%2221st_Building%22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention of Zlín is from 1322, when it was acquired by Queen Elizabeth Richeza. It was already a market town and a craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> centre for the surrounding region of Moravian Wallachia. From 1358 the estate belonged to Bishop Albrecht of Šternberk and became the seat of the Moravian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sternberg">Šternberk family</a>, and in 1397 its town privileges were extended. The Hussite Wars forced the family to sell it in 1437. The 16th century brought peace and prosperity, with drapery, pottery and shoemaking flourishing.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-bata">Tomáš Baťa</a> and his siblings founded a shoe factory in 1894. By 1922 it was the leading maker of footwear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and the company moved into engineering, chemistry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> technology. Baťa was elected mayor in 1923. Between 1923 and 1938 the company built a large complex on the outskirts with thousands of flats, schools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> stores, scientific facilities and a hospital, in a controlled development shaped by modern urban ideas. The plan was the work of František Lydie Gahura, who had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a>'s atelier, and the city drew on both Ebenezer Howard's garden city and Le Corbusier's vision of urban modernity.</p><p>Buildings of the period include Baťa's Hospital of 1927, the Grand Cinema of 1932, with 2,270 seats the largest cinema in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> in its time, and the Tomas Bata Memorial of 1933. Baťa's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">Skyscraper</a>, built in 1936–1939 to a design by Vladimír Karfík, rose 77.5 m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-bata">Tomáš Baťa</a> died in 1932 and the company was run by others. German occupation halted development in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and in 1944 American bombing destroyed large parts of the factories. Soviet and Romanian armies liberated the city on 2 May 1945, and in October 1945 the Bata company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was nationalised. The city name returned to Zlín on 1 January 1990. Zlín has about 75,000 inhabitants, lies on the Dřevnice River about 70 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, is the seat of the Zlín Region and has been home to Tomas Bata University since 2001. Much of its centre is protected as an urban monument zone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zlín” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Czech lands had been the industrial heart of the Habsburg monarchy, and Czechoslovakia inherited a large share of its industry, from the Škoda Works at Plzeň to textile, glass, sugar and brewing concerns, while Tomáš Baťa built a world-scale shoe business at <b>Zlín</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
